<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Whalley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1607–c. 1675</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Whalley was an English Parliamentarian cavalry commander in the Civil War, a cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>, and one of the judges who signed the death warrant of Charles I in 1649. At the Restoration in 1660 he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> with his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-goffe">William Goffe</a>. Sheltered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a> in the New Haven Colony and later at Hadley, Massachusetts, they evaded royal agents, and he died in hiding.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-whalley.jpg" alt="Edward Whalley" width="260" height="96" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward Whalley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Signature_of_Edward_Whalley.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
